/**
 * STAGING / DEPLOY (GDD §12.6, §3.1) — the drop-pod launch console.
 *
 * The first real screen of the game. The commander is in orbit with no ground,
 * no column and no yield, looking down at a contested world through a targeting
 * reticle. Everything on this screen exists to answer one question honestly:
 * *where should the pod come down?*
 *
 *   · a live `MapScene` underneath, framed by an orbital veil
 *   · a fixed centre reticle that continuously re-acquires the hex beneath it,
 *     so panning the world IS aiming the pod
 *   · vertical telemetry rails — offered sites on the left, active sectors on
 *     the right — both tappable, both real data
 *   · a target dossier: terrain, per-tick yield, gas, adjacency, nearest rival,
 *     and the sector's SHUTDOWN clock, because dropping into a sector that
 *     rotates out is the single worst opening move in the game (GDD §3.2)
 *   · SCAN FOR SITES, which ranks legal ground and *says why* it likes it
 *   · a guild nudge for the unaffiliated, reusing the real charter flow
 *   · an arming sequence with weight behind the irreversible commit
 *
 * Scene lifecycle differs deliberately from screens/map.ts. The map is the home
 * screen and is bounced in and out of constantly, so it parks its context on an
 * idle timer. Staging is a one-way door — the shell routes straight to the map
 * the instant the drop lands — so holding the context past `unmount()` would
 * guarantee the exact overlap it was meant to avoid. It releases on unmount,
 * the way the battle screen does; two live WebGL contexts on a phone is a
 * crash, not a slowdown.
 */
